            <h3>Attackers&#x27; AI Agents Found a Zero-Day in Medical-Imaging Software and Gained Root Access</h3>
            <p>Suspected China-linked hackers exploited a zero-day in MONAI Label to gain root access via an unauthenticated file write vulnerability.</p>
            <p>Suspected China-linked hackers used AI agents to find a zero-day in MONAI Label and gain root access to a university server. The flaw allows unauthenticated users to write files outside the storage folder. This affects versions 0.8.5 and earlier when running without login.</p>
            <p>The agents mapped the application interface and wrote files in about 11 minutes. They used the same flaw to plant a file that Python runs at startup. This triggered a training feature that loaded the file and connected back to the attacker as root.</p>
            <p>The maintainers fixed the source code but no patched version is available. The flaw is now listed as CVE-2026-133968 with a critical rating. The practical steps are to block untrusted access, enable authentication, and run the server under a limited account.</p>
            <p>We need stronger governance and security controls for agentic AI systems. These systems can autonomously discover and exploit vulnerabilities. This highlights the need for robust AI governance and zero-trust principles in AI operations.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-31235">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-21">21 November 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>A Single Malicious Webpage Let Researchers Hijack Meta Muse and Access Private Chats</h3>
            <p>A malicious webpage could hijack Meta Muse and access private user data by exploiting browser and kernel vulnerabilities.</p>
            <p>A single malicious webpage could hijack Meta Muse and access private user data without triggering any approval prompts. The researchers demonstrated how an attacker could extract conversation history and stored memories from the agent&#x27;s cloud machine. This shows how even well-protected systems can have blind spots when it comes to AI agents and their interactions with external content.</p>
            <p>The attack chain exploited a flaw in Muse&#x27;s built-in browser and a Linux kernel vulnerability to gain full control of the user&#x27;s cloud environment. This reached beyond the agent&#x27;s intended boundaries, including the credential store and the Sentinel monitoring layer. It highlights how critical it is to secure the entire stack, especially when dealing with agentic AI that operates on cloud infrastructure.</p>
            <p>Sentinel was designed as the final gatekeeper for outbound traffic, but the attack bypassed it entirely. This underscores the need for continuous monitoring and strict access controls. Even with built-in security measures, the attack path shows how quickly an adversary can exploit weaknesses in the chain of trust.</p>
            <p>The research team worked with Meta to fix the vulnerabilities before publishing. This collaboration is a positive sign, but it also reminds us that security is an ongoing process. As AI agents become more integrated into our digital lives, we must ensure they&#x27;re as secure as the systems they interact with.</p>
          </article>
